The `implicit-auth-redirect` endpoint returns 200 (not a redirect) — it's likely a client-side JS handler that reads the token from the URL fragment and then does `window.location = redirect`. Since it's SPAs/implicit flow, the `redirect=` param is processed client-side. That means an attacker-controlled `redirect=` would be honored client-side (an open redirect in the SPA that could redirect the token-carrying fragment to attacker). But client-side redirect is only exploitable if open redirect lands on attacker domain carrying the fragment — classic **OAuth implicit token leak via open redirect**.

